{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**Diligence checklist—not a universal notice period or guaranteed right to substitute**\n\n- **Licence mechanics:** Confirm term, renewal/extension process, fees and escalators, permitted use (fund name, ticker, marketing, website, prospectus, shareholder reports, performance history and derivatives), territory, data/API access, audit rights, sublicensing to service providers, confidentiality, and any limits on tracking or replication.\n- **Termination and renewal failure:** Identify expiry versus early-termination triggers, cure periods, notice and delivery requirements, transition/cooperation duties, accrued-fee obligations, data access during wind-down, and rights to continue using the index name or historical data. Check change-of-control, assignment, insolvency, force-majeure and unilateral amendment provisions. Do not assume a standard notice window or post-expiry grace period.\n- **Contingency authority:** Review trust documents, investment objective/policies, exemptive relief and disclosure for authority and process to change the index, adviser, name, ticker or strategy—including board, shareholder, exchange and SEC actions that may be required. Assess feasibility, timing, costs, tracking, tax and operational impacts; do not assume substitution is contractually or regulatorily automatic.\n- **Naming dependencies:** Map index-provider trademarks and naming rules across fund/legal name, series, ticker, share class, domain, branding and marketing. Determine whether continued use requires a separate licence and how quickly names/materials must change.\n- **Affiliated-information conflicts:** Identify provider/adviser/issuer affiliations and information barriers. Examine access to unpublished index changes, constituent or rebalance data, confidentiality, trading restrictions, conflicts controls, recordkeeping and escalation—especially if an affiliate manages, trades or competes with the ETF."}}
